  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Concept Check 4.1\n",
    "\n",
    "Random Forest achieves R² = 0.702 on this housing data, 12 percentage points better than linear models. True or false: This means macro economic indicators (unemployment, income, etc.) don't matter for prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "A) True - if they mattered, linear models would capture them\n",
    "B) False - macro indicators are already included in the Random Forest features\n",
    "C) False - Random Forest may simply capture non-linearity better than linear models\n",
    "D) True - tree models ignore economic data\n",
    "\n",
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary>Answer</summary>\n",
    "B and C are both true. Macro indicators ARE included in the Random Forest features (same data as linear model), so they contribute to the improvement. But the improvement comes primarily from non-linearity (e.g., diminishing returns on size, interaction effects) rather than macro data specifically. Macro data helps both models, but non-linearity detection helps Random Forest more.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
